#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100660 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6c44b63d-72f8-599a-a89c-29fdc2227ab5" :question/math-academy-id "e-357" :question/problem "A company spends $C(x) = x^2 + 80x$ dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product. The company sells the product for $\\[math] 180$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $60$ units of product per month?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the company has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=180\\cdot x=180 x$ dollars. \nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =180x-({x}^{2}+80x) \\\\  & =100x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the question statement, we know that the permitted domain is $0\\leq x \\leq 60.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 < x < 60.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x)=100-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 100-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=50$ lies in the interval $0 <  x < 60.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=50$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 <           0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=50$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=50$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(50) & =100(50)-(50)^{2}=\\text{\\[}math]2\\,500.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100661 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1aca5854-177d-5ebe-9a63-caaa7d916c4d" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing a Profit, Revenue, or Cost Function"}] :question/id #uuid "e47dca19-6772-532a-9955-ebe6f3db0d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-359" :question/problem "The director of a newspaper estimates that the company's profit, in hundreds of dollars, is given by \n\n$$\nP(x) = -x^2 + 44x + 5,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $x$ is the number of newspapers, in thousands, produced daily. Given that the company has the capacity to produce $30\\,000$ newspapers every day, how many newspapers per day should the company produce to maximize profit?" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the permitted domain is \n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 30,\n$$\n\n since $x = 30$ corresponds to $30 \\cdot 1\\,000 = 30\\,000$ copies of the newspaper. \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 <       x <       30.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = -2x + 44,\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -2x+44 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =44 \\\\ x & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=22$ lies in the interval $0 \\leq x \\leq 30.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=22$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 < 0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=22$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=22$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the company should produce $22 \\cdot 1\\,000 = 22\\,000$ copies of the newspaper per day to maximize its profit."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100784 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a63d3bdb-ca20-51f5-b876-7ec8950a46dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-7574" :question/problem "A hotel has $21$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $\\[math]12$ per night,  all rooms are occupied and, for every $\\[/math]1$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance of each rented room is $\\[math]3$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit, and what is the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(21-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(21-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =252+21x-12x-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =252+9x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(21-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =3(21-x) \\\\  & =63-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(252+9x-{x}^{2})-(63-3x) \\\\  & =189+12x-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are $21$ rooms, the permitted domain is $0 \\leq x\\leq 21.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0 \\lt x\\lt 21.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nP'(x) = 12 - 2x \\,.\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12-2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=6$ lies in the interval $0 <  x < 21.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\nP''(x) = -2 <             0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=6$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit when there are $x=6$ vacant rooms.  \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(12+x)$ dollars. If there are $6$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12+6 & =\\text{\\[}math]18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $\\[/math]18$ per night."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100788 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2d19236-d6ea-57da-b2a4-b926fdddb30e" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Revenue Function Given a Price Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7996afa5-da25-51e5-915c-5f0acdccb8bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-7697" :question/problem "A textile center sells $x$ hundreds of meters of fabric at a price $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x) = x^2 - 15x + 48 + \\dfrac{78}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the textile center sells between $100$ and $900$ meters of fabric daily, how many meters of fabric must the textile center sell to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ hundreds of meters in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-15x+48+\\frac{78}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+48x+78.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that the permitted domain is $1 \\leq x \\leq 9,$ since $x = 1$ corresponds to $1 \\cdot 100 = 100$ and $x = 9$ corresponds to $9 \\cdot 100 = 900.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=9$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1 <         x <         9.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\nR'(x) = 3x^2 - 30x + 48,\n$$\n\nand solving $R'(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+48 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+16 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=2$ and $x=8.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,2, 8$ and $9.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-15(1)^{2}+48(1)+78=112 \\\\ R(2) & =(2)^{3}-15(2)^{2}+48(2)+78=122\\,✓ \\\\ R(8) & =(8)^{3}-15(8)^{2}+48(8)+78=14 \\\\ R(9) & =(9)^{3}-15(9)^{2}+48(9)+78=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the textile center should sell $2 \\cdot 100 = 200$ meters of fabric."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1aca5854-177d-5ebe-9a63-caaa7d916c4d" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing a Profit, Revenue, or Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25bc201d-66ea-53fc-9b0e-c3e2d40079a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,750" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510862} {:answer/id #uuid "39f3400f-def2-582b-b868-8b4f355233c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510913} {:answer/id #uuid "a36a5800-821a-5eb0-99da-dfc32bfe6ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "375" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511125} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5e05a4-3c93-5c87-834b-f68d2820cbc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511205} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c37437-ce26-5498-8d60-f163e1765a66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25bc201d-66ea-53fc-9b0e-c3e2d40079a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,750" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510862} :answer-field/id #uuid "96c86f48-8fdd-5608-aed8-3ae8938afdea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "300985dc-198c-5051-b5a4-3809b4e56dd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-119010" :question/problem "A coffee producer company estimates that the profit of its coffee production, in dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-0.002{x}^{2}+3.9x-3,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ are the tens of kilograms of coffee produced daily. Given that the company can produce at most $10\\,000$ kilograms per day, how many kilograms should the company produce per day to maximize its daily profit?" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ represents the number of tens of kilograms of coffee produced daily, and since the company produces at most $10\\,000$ kilograms per day, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1\\,000.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<1\\,000.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-0.004x+3.9,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -0.004x+3.9 & =0 \\\\ x & =975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=975$ lies in the interval $0<x<1\\,000.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=975$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.004<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=975$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=975$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the company should produce $975\\cdot 10=9\\,750$ kilograms of coffee per day to maximize its daily profit."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2d19236-d6ea-57da-b2a4-b926fdddb30e" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Revenue Function Given a Price Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "080330c5-a425-5e6c-b28c-e7be16c7d10c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "820" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510796} {:answer/id #uuid "3d538af1-335c-51bd-b85a-93ea9a161e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510923} {:answer/id #uuid "600e5b0c-e158-5ee6-8730-7bcf0774f0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "700" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510985} {:answer/id #uuid "94bc2a7c-6ef5-5e08-91ef-2c4935fbdb11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "540" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511089} {:answer/id #uuid "a464b164-3d5e-5265-8432-b765f2245c04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d538af1-335c-51bd-b85a-93ea9a161e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510923} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f83acf-0c32-5e5e-8073-81cec7cfab01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "397e9b41-cb22-58e3-8777-87698586bfce" :question/math-academy-id "q-119021" :question/problem "A clothes store sells $x$ hundreds of pants at a price $p(x),$  in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)=200+40x-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf the store sells between $500$ and $1\\,000$ pants per month, how many pants should it sell monthly to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ pants, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x(200+40x-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =200x+40{x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $5\\le x\\le 10,$  since $x=5$  corresponds to $5\\cdot 100=500$  and $x=10$ corresponds to $10\\cdot 100=1\\,000.$  Therefore, $x=5$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $5<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=200+80x-10{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{2}-80x-200 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x-20 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-10) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-2$ and $x=10.$ However, the value $x=-2$  is not in the permitted domain. Moreover, $x=10$ is already a candidate, so our candidates are  $x=5$ and $x=10.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(5) & =200(5)+40(5)^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{5}^{3}≈1\\,583 \\\\ R(10) & =200(10)+40(10)^{2}-\\frac{10}{3}{10}^{3}≈2\\,667\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the clothes store should sell $1\\,000$ pants monthly."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2d19236-d6ea-57da-b2a4-b926fdddb30e" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Revenue Function Given a Price Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dde279e-504b-5975-b666-d6e44cfae064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510877} {:answer/id #uuid "469492a5-d663-54f3-a329-e35b77e8e445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510941} {:answer/id #uuid "9d81dc2a-f44f-5831-8528-42073dd5e870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511111} {:answer/id #uuid "e826da33-9921-51cd-a0e7-44f6662e2dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511267} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf1c2ad-b715-5a9e-82cc-eb26e807f1e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dde279e-504b-5975-b666-d6e44cfae064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510877} :answer-field/id #uuid "732a4786-9390-5f34-af22-8bd5a4c708b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83c84e0b-efbe-5ed1-89f6-e89dc6ca66e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-119028" :question/problem "A school is organizing a trip to a theme park. The trip is offered to at least $12$ kids, and it can be attended by at most $20$ kids. The cost of each ticket is $$80$ if there are exactly $12$ kids. If there are more than $12$ kids, the price per ticket drops by $$4$ for each kid in excess of $12.$ Every kid pays the same amount for going on the trip.\n\nHow many kids must go on the trip for the theme park to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $12+x$ kids, then the price per ticket is $80-4x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(12+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of kids}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{(80-4x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{price per ticket}} \\\\  & =(12+x)(80-4x) \\\\  & =960-48x+80x-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =960+32x-4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there can only be between $12$ and $20$ kids, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & \\le x+12\\le 20 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=8$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<8.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=32-8x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 32-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x & =32 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=4$ lies in the interval $0<x<8.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=4$ does indeed give a maximum value of $R(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=4$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=4$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the theme park should admit $12+4=16$ kids to maximize its revenue."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7d727ea-08a3-55dd-a017-4d107ec1aa2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098014} {:answer/id #uuid "e882f15d-cbb3-58de-8db6-593afd851087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098015} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9cdd31-c47f-5335-b161-947bd2eae8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098016} {:answer/id #uuid "51adfce4-2819-5700-8925-9a7b4a6d258d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098017} {:answer/id #uuid "5755991d-f45e-58ee-b0da-3896a698d7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a9cdd31-c47f-5335-b161-947bd2eae8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098016} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c185337-13a1-5e13-a931-af896063703c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "284fac66-d855-554d-969c-bb28dda1de3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-119091" :question/problem "A summer camp has capacity for $200$ kids. The price for each kid attending the camp is $$300.$ The cost of setting up the camp is made up of a fixed cost of $$2\\,000$ plus a cost of $$100$ per kid. How many kids should attend the summer camp to maximize its profit?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ kids attending, the summer camp has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=300\\cdot x=300x\n$$\n\nand a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nC(x)={{2\\,000}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}\\,\\,+{{100x.}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =300x-(2\\,000+100x) \\\\  & =200x-2\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 200.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=200$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<200.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=200.\n$$\n\nWe can see that the equation ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ has no solutions.\n\nThus, our candidates are $x=0$ and $x=200.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =200(0)-2\\,000=-2\\,000 \\\\ P(200) & =200(200)-2\\,000=38\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the summer camp will have a maximum profit level when $200$ kids attend."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e2d9015-7c6e-5b67-9a9c-095a3636dc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$6.7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510981} {:answer/id #uuid "5fcc83c3-0427-5087-ba48-9b12c96afdf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.65$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510984} {:answer/id #uuid "795b1cfc-a40e-577c-9727-d8a557ce295a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$6.3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511031} {:answer/id #uuid "c7738231-00cd-5bfb-9cbd-70ba13d1a6eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1.85$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511196} {:answer/id #uuid "fb06f779-6b7c-57da-960c-d293832340e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e2d9015-7c6e-5b67-9a9c-095a3636dc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$6.7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510981} :answer-field/id #uuid "15bd47fc-61d9-56ee-8807-cdce49006fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8babed00-8ea0-55fe-9919-358ba07b0746" :question/math-academy-id "q-119098" :question/problem "A coffee shop can produce up to $500$ cups of coffee per day. When the price per cup of coffee is $$3,$ the coffee shop sells all of them, and, for each dollar increase in the price, $50$ cups remain unsold. If the cost for producing a cup of coffee is $$0.40,$ what price per cup should be set to obtain the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ hundreds of *unsold* cups of coffee, the coffee shop sells $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, and the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in hundreds of dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(5-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{sold cups of coffee}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(3+2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{price per cup}} \\\\  & =15+10x-3x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =15+7x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for selling $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, the coffee shop has a cost (in hundreds of dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =0.4(5-x) \\\\  & =2-0.4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(15+7x-2{x}^{2})-(2-0.4x) \\\\  & =13+7.4x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 5.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<5.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=7.4-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 7.4-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =7.4 \\\\ x & =1.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1.85$ lies in the interval $0<x<5.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1.85$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1.85$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=1.85$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the coffee shop will have a maximum profit level when there are $185$ unsold cups of coffee.  \n\nWe know that the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. If there are $1.85$ hundreds of unsold cups of coffee, then the price per cup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2(1.85) & =$6.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coffee shop should sell each cup of coffee for $$6.7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1aca5854-177d-5ebe-9a63-caaa7d916c4d" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing a Profit, Revenue, or Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d97af0b-7e54-5d34-9126-07d44330312c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510924} {:answer/id #uuid "c0858bb9-0eb9-573d-9bea-d595cf1d7421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511178} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8ead9f-c9de-5346-ac44-db1b6b8f2faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511238} {:answer/id #uuid "e83b28f6-aa90-51cc-9eba-ba8f1cc6231d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511268} {:answer/id #uuid "f36cae25-67e6-5172-9f1f-88f47f2ea096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e83b28f6-aa90-51cc-9eba-ba8f1cc6231d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511268} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f434994-c85a-56bb-adb3-a969e9d9b29b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7844b31-dcef-5082-a108-686c79e5bf57" :question/math-academy-id "q-15324" :question/problem "A fast-food chain wants to open some new restaurants. The company estimates that its annual profit, in hundreds of thousands of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-12x+20,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of new restaurants it opens up. If the company has a budget to open up between $1$ and $8$ restaurants, how many restaurants should the company open to maximize its profit?" :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ represents the number of  new restaurants opened, and since the company can open between $1$ and $8$ restaurants, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 8.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<8.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-{x}^{2}+8x-12.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-2x+8\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=6$ into ${P}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(2) & =-2(2)+8=4>0, \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =-2(6)+8=-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative at $x=6,$ the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=6$ and $x=8.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+4(1)^{2}-12(1)+20=\\frac{35}{3}≈12 \\\\ P(6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}+4(6)^{2}-12(6)+20=20\\,✓ \\\\ P(8) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{3}+4(8)^{2}-12(8)+20=\\frac{28}{3}≈9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is the global maximum of $P(x).$ So, the company must open $6$ restaurants to maximize its profit."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "435b2373-d036-5a72-a5b3-9b22809d8383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$50$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510933} {:answer/id #uuid "67c15ab1-b48d-50a4-92c6-9e51aa87b1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$84$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511002} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a572cf-f451-578e-b062-948286b9f2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$98$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511133} {:answer/id #uuid "d26f525d-acc9-5080-9da8-6d1c7218b145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$120$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511216} {:answer/id #uuid "e464d60b-ec47-553e-aef7-bedba5f73485" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$106$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6a572cf-f451-578e-b062-948286b9f2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$98$ per night" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511133} :answer-field/id #uuid "97e14268-ee5f-5656-86a1-d067419325eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2888ed58-7e1b-57a5-91ea-2124b7fe040e" :question/math-academy-id "q-18671" :question/problem "A hotel has $71$ rooms. The manager has observed that when the room rate is $$50$ per night, all rooms are occupied and, for every $$2$ increase in the rate, a single room is vacated. If the maintenance per rented room is $$4$ per night, what rate per room should the manager set to obtain the maximum profit?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ *vacant* rooms, the hotel has $(71-x)$ occupied rooms, and the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(71-x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{occupied rooms}}\\times \\,\\,\\,{{(50+2x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{rate per room}} \\\\  & =3\\,550+142x-50x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for having $(71-x)$ rooms rented out, the hotel has a cost (in dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =4(71-x) \\\\  & =284-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =3\\,550+92x-2{x}^{2}-(284-4x) \\\\  & =3\\,266+96x-2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 71.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<71$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=96-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 96-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =96 \\\\ x & =24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=24$ lies in the interval $0<x<71.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=24$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=24$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=24$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the hotel will have a maximum profit level when there are $x=24$ vacant rooms. \n\nWe know that the rate per room is $(50+2x)$ dollars. If there are $24$ vacant rooms, then the rate per room is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50+2(24) & =$98.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the manager should charge a room rate of $$98$ per night."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca141565-3816-5092-880f-28f10de87eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca141565-3816-5092-880f-28f10de87eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511201} :answer-field/id #uuid "baf6f008-070c-539f-b70e-a2e12580def6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eefd8d60-720e-5861-a3ae-27d7c6377bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-316188" :question/problem "A theme park sells ride passes each day. The price of each ride pass is $$15.$ The cost of operating the park includes a fixed daily cost of $$700$ and a variable cost of $$2$ per ride pass sold. If the park can sell at most $500$ ride passes per day, what is the number of ride passes that maximizes the park's profit?\n\nThe number of ride passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ ride passes per day, the park has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=15x.\n$$\n\nThe cost (in dollars) of operating the park is made up of a fixed cost and a variable cost:\n\n$$\nC(x)={{700}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}+{{2x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =15x-(700+2x) \\\\  & =13x-700.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 500.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=500$ are both candidates for the global maximum.\n\nNext, we take the derivative of the profit function:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=13,\n$$\n\nwhich is constant and positive. Since ${P}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x,$ the profit function is increasing throughout the domain.\n\nThus, the maximum profit occurs at the endpoint $x=500.$ Let’s confirm by computing values at both endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =13(0)-700=-700, \\\\ P(500) & =13(500)-700=6500-700=5800\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of ride passes that maximizes the park’s profit is $500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1aca5854-177d-5ebe-9a63-caaa7d916c4d" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing a Profit, Revenue, or Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce0f7359-5ce8-5e71-89e3-480ebd8e48f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce0f7359-5ce8-5e71-89e3-480ebd8e48f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511209} :answer-field/id #uuid "96e90f42-a40e-5675-933f-77aa92860dd4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f837bd1-8f94-5b36-a5b6-46a5ca94e710" :question/math-academy-id "q-346940" :question/problem "The manager of a bakery estimates that the profit of the bakery, in dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-2{x}^{2}+120x-1\\,500,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of loaves baked daily. Given that the bakery can bake at most $400$ loaves per day, how many loaves should the bakery bake per day to maximize its daily profit?\n\nThe bakery should bake\n{{field-1}} loaves per day to maximize its daily profit." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ represents the number of loaves baked daily and the bakery can bake at most $400$ loaves per day, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 400.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<400.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-4x+120,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -4x+120 & =0 \\\\ x & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=30$ lies in the interval $0<x<400.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=30$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=30$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=30$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$\n\nTherefore, the bakery should bake $30$ loaves per day to maximize its daily profit."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1aca5854-177d-5ebe-9a63-caaa7d916c4d" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing a Profit, Revenue, or Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fa2cd2b-240f-554e-bc3d-a20b62887645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fa2cd2b-240f-554e-bc3d-a20b62887645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511015} :answer-field/id #uuid "612e0cf9-0fec-5ac7-8aa7-de4c56b9b84c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "626a28da-89e6-52c5-8bca-1f554918b829" :question/math-academy-id "q-346947" :question/problem "Upon launching a new online course, the chief financial officer (CFO) of a company estimates that its profit, in thousands of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=0.3{x}^{3}-3.6{x}^{2}+10.8x+5,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the price, in dollars, of a single course. If the CFO estimates that the course price should be between $$1$ and $$7,$ at what price is the maximum profit obtained?\n\nThe profit is maximum when a single course costs $$$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $x$ represents the price of a single course and since the CFO estimates that the course price should be between $$1$ and $$7,$  the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 7.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<7.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=0.9{x}^{2}-7.2x+10.8.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 0.9{x}^{2}-7.2x+10.8 & =0 \\\\ 0.9({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ 0.9(x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=1.8x-7.2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=6$ into ${P}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(2) & =1.8(2)-7.2=-3.6<0, \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =1.8(6)-7.2=3.6>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative at $x=2,$ the point $x=2$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2$ and $x=7.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =0.3(1)^{3}-3.6(1)^{2}+10.8(1)+5=12.5 \\\\ P(2) & =0.3(2)^{3}-3.6(2)^{2}+10.8(2)+5=14.6\\,✓ \\\\ P(7) & =0.3(7)^{3}-3.6(7)^{2}+10.8(7)+5=7.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is the global maximum of $P(x).$ So, the profit is maximum when a single course costs $$\\,2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101017 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1aca5854-177d-5ebe-9a63-caaa7d916c4d" :knowledge-point/title "Optimizing a Profit, Revenue, or Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60260afa-81b2-5401-a999-9c5593207039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60260afa-81b2-5401-a999-9c5593207039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510986} :answer-field/id #uuid "cecc26af-aa31-562c-ae17-33a4a09196f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bb428e79-eefe-5650-9407-e82260832c0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346953" :question/problem "The cost for a company to produce $x$ bicycles, in hundreds of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nC(x)={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+48x+300.\n$$\n\nGiven that the company can produce at most $10$ bicycles per week, how many bicycles should the company produce per week to minimize its weekly cost?\n\nThe company should produce\n{{field-1}}\nbicycles per week to minimize its weekly cost." :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ represent the number of bicycles produced per week. Since the company can produce at most $10$ bicycles per week, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 10.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $C(x)$ for $0<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-30x+48,\n$$\n\nand solving ${C}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+48 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-10x+16) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)(x-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=8$ gives a minimum value of $C(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=6x-30\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=8$ into ${C}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{″}(2) & =12-30=-18<0, \\\\ {C}^{″}(8) & =48-30=18>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive at $x=8,$ the point $x=8$ is a local minimum of $C(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=8$ and $x=10.$ Computing $C(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(0) & =300 \\\\ C(8) & =(8)^{3}-15(8)^{2}+48(8)+300=236\\,✓ \\\\ C(10) & =(10)^{3}-15(10)^{2}+48(10)+300=280\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=8$ is the global minimum of $C(x),$ and the company should produce $8$ bicycles per week to minimize weekly cost."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2d19236-d6ea-57da-b2a4-b926fdddb30e" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Revenue Function Given a Price Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51dff2bc-6adb-539d-8c94-2b1ff31cd0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51dff2bc-6adb-539d-8c94-2b1ff31cd0fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510958} :answer-field/id #uuid "277fac87-c14e-510e-81e5-ecb4ba9ef95c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0e359a4-15e8-5a4f-83b1-ea0f9d507e49" :question/math-academy-id "q-346992" :question/problem "A photography studio books $x$ photo sessions per day at a price per session $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2}-9x+15+\\frac{45}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the studio books between $1$ and $6$ sessions per day, how many sessions should it book to maximize its revenue?\n\nTo maximize revenue, the studio should book\n{{field-1}}\nphoto sessions per day." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-9x+15+\\frac{45}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+15x+45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-18x+15,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-18x+15 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+5 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1$ and $x=5.$ However, $x=1$ is already a candidate, so the new candidate is $x=5.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,\\,5,$ and $6.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-9(1)^{2}+15(1)+45=1-9+15+45=52\\,✓ \\\\ R(5) & =(5)^{3}-9(5)^{2}+15(5)+45=125-225+75+45=20 \\\\ R(6) & =(6)^{3}-9(6)^{2}+15(6)+45=216-324+90+45=27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize revenue, the studio should book $1$ photo session per day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2d19236-d6ea-57da-b2a4-b926fdddb30e" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Revenue Function Given a Price Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0052cd3e-3060-5730-98da-8af2605187af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0052cd3e-3060-5730-98da-8af2605187af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510773} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ae518f5-b8f5-5525-bcc5-e90c4f502064" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ae7f739-7e2b-55d5-873a-c21108d944ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-347034" :question/problem "A private art studio can enroll between $8$ and $14$ students in a painting workshop. The fee that each student pays is $$72$ if exactly $8$ students enroll, and if more than $8$ students are enrolled, the fee drops by $$4$ for each additional student. Each student pays the same fee.\n\nHow many students must the workshop have to maximize its revenue?\n\nTo maximize its revenue, the workshop must have\n{{field-1}}\nstudents." :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $8+x$ students, then the fee is $72-4x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={{(8+x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{number of students}}\\times {{(72-4x)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fee}} \\\\  & =(8+x)(72-4x) \\\\  & =576-32x+72x-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =576+40x-4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the workshop can only have between $8$ and $14$ students, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n8\\le x+8\\le 14⇒0\\le x\\le 6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=40-8x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 40-8x & =0 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=5$ lies in the domain $0<x<6.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=5$ gives a maximum. To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-8<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=5$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=5$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the workshop must have $8+5=13$ students enrolled to maximize its revenue."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511520 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fbeab81-4eff-5381-a265-fe79b799fdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fbeab81-4eff-5381-a265-fe79b799fdee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510852} :answer-field/id #uuid "167a66ac-c75b-5fb3-804d-27eb25cf3aa4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "896ab313-4af7-5cfc-9d60-946032fe5a62" :question/math-academy-id "q-347242" :question/problem "A plant spends $C(x)=150+20x+24{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}$ thousands of dollars to produce $x$ units of a certain product per month. The plant sells the product for $$200\\,000$ per unit. Given that the plant can produce between $2$ and $9$ units per month, how many units should the plant produce per month to maximize its profit?\n\nThe optimal number of plant production is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product, the plant has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=200\\cdot x=200x$ thousands of dollars.\n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =200x-(150+20x+24{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+180x-150.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $2\\le x\\le 9.$ Therefore, $x=2$ and $x=9$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $2<x<9.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-48x+180,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-48x+180 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-16x+60 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-10) & =0 \\\\ x & =6,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the critical points $x=6$ and $x=10,$ only $x=6$ is within the permitted domain.\n\nAll that's left now is to check if $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(x) & =6x-48 \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =-12<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative at $x=6$ is negative, the point $x=6$ is indeed a maximum.\n\nOur candidates are $x=2,x=6$ and $x=9.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =(2)^{3}-24(2)^{2}+180(2)-150=122 \\\\ P(6) & =(6)^{3}-24(6)^{2}+180(6)-150=282\\,✓ \\\\ P(9) & =(9)^{3}-24(9)^{2}+180(9)-150=255\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the plant will have a maximum profit level when it produces $6$ units per month."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2b9bdb1-70a2-566d-88d8-367ecbac51d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1296" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2b9bdb1-70a2-566d-88d8-367ecbac51d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1296" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511151} :answer-field/id #uuid "888e6519-6f88-5145-b927-7bc3da422884" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de3d69db-3561-564d-a214-9ac683352f20" :question/math-academy-id "q-347261" :question/problem "A candle company spends $C(x)=120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}$ dollars to produce $x$ candles per week. The company sells each candle for $$120.$ Given that the company can produce between $1$ and $6$ candles per week, what is the maximum weekly profit that the company can make?\n\nThe maximum weekly profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ candles, the company has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=120\\cdot x=120x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =120x-(120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-16{x}^{3}+3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 12x(4-4x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 12x(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ However, the value $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=2.$\n\nLet's check if $x=2$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=48-96x+36{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(2)=48-192+144=0,\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm that $x=2$ is a maximum. Therefore, we must include $x=2$ as a candidate.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=6.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =24(1)^{2}-16(1)^{3}+3(1)^{4}=11 \\\\ P(2) & =24(2)^{2}-16(2)^{3}+3(2)^{4}=32 \\\\ P(6) & =24(6)^{2}-16(6)^{3}+3(6)^{4}=1296\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum weekly profit that the company can make is $$\\,1296.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dedbf55-c0ea-588a-a30e-090c0bd91a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dedbf55-c0ea-588a-a30e-090c0bd91a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510977} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3b3f22b-6d4c-5cc5-9130-cf5e72b4c48c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7b5b31e-6d04-57eb-9051-cd4496c07c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-347264" :question/problem "A factory spends  $C(x)=0.002375{x}^{2}+0.3x+1\\,000$ dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $30-\\frac{x}{100}$ dollars per unit. Given that the factory can produce at most $2\\,000$ units per year, how many units should the factory produce per year to maximize its annual profit?\n\nThe optimal number of production is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per year, the factory has an annual revenue (income) of $R(x)=(30-\\frac{x}{100})\\cdot x=30x-0.01{x}^{2}$ dollars.\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =30x-0.01{x}^{2}-(0.002375{x}^{2}+0.3x+1\\,000) \\\\  & =-1\\,000+29.7x-0.012375{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 2\\,000.$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<2\\,000.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=29.7-0.02475x.\n$$\n\nSolving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 29.7-0.02475x & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1\\,200$ lies in the interval $0<x<2\\,000.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1\\,200$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:   \n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-0.02475<0.\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1\\,200$ is indeed a  maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=1\\,200,$ and $x=2\\,000.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =-1\\,000 \\\\ P(1\\,200) & =-1\\,000+29.7(1\\,200)-0.012375(1\\,200)^{2}=16\\,820\\,✓ \\\\ P(2\\,000) & =-1\\,000+29.7(2\\,000)-0.012375(2\\,000)^{2}=9\\,500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the factory will have a maximum annual profit when it produces $1\\,200$ units per year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101020 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6d2b1036-0ea4-5fd1-bfed-885dcbae618b" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function by Constructing a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b400b302-93f5-5cab-a964-15b179561edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b400b302-93f5-5cab-a964-15b179561edb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511154} :answer-field/id #uuid "10db67e6-511a-52b3-adcc-8dc9f9185159" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "244652f5-cacd-5369-8e7e-0fc003669027" :question/math-academy-id "q-347272" :question/problem "A museum sells daily entry passes. The price of each pass is $$20.$ The cost of operating the museum includes a fixed daily cost of $$1,100$ and a variable cost of $$5$ per pass sold. If the museum can sell at most $300$ passes per day, what is the number of passes that maximizes the museum's profit?\n\nThe number of passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ passes per day, the museum has a revenue (in dollars) of \n\n$$\nR(x)=20x.\n$$\n\nThe cost (in dollars) of operating the museum is made up of a fixed cost and a variable cost:\n\n$$\nC(x)={{1100}_{⏟}}_{\\text{fixed cost}}+{{5x}_{⏟}}_{\\text{variable cost}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =20x-(1100+5x) \\\\  & =15x-1100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 300.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=300$ are both candidates for the global maximum.\n\nNext, we take the derivative of the profit function:\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=15,\n$$\n\nwhich is constant and positive. Since ${P}^{′}(x)>0$ for all $x,$ the profit function is increasing throughout the domain.\n\nThus, the maximum profit occurs at the endpoint $x=300.$ Let’s confirm by computing values at both endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =-1100, \\\\ P(300) & =15(300)-1100=4500-1100=3400\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of passes that maximizes the museum’s profit is $300.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17aa9f4f-ce84-554a-bc96-221077334d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$243\\,000.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510834} {:answer/id #uuid "933d6611-667a-577f-a7e0-6fa58e2da1ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$176\\,000.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511084} {:answer/id #uuid "9974f26a-f280-5eb2-933d-048ae35c522b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$310\\,000.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511101} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c2b6a7-709f-5948-a5f8-ebe843858f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$107\\,000.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511227} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbf2bc7-cb32-5dcc-bad6-c8c2e13465b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$200\\,000.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17aa9f4f-ce84-554a-bc96-221077334d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$243\\,000.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510834} :answer-field/id #uuid "8efadd76-a965-516a-bd05-82f7d6c9e07e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5c03e3f-902a-5f23-a083-877f778bea41" :question/math-academy-id "q-65867" :question/problem "A factory spends $C(x)={x}^{4}+13x$ thousand dollars to manufacture $x$ units of a certain product. The factory sells the product for $$121\\,000$ per unit. What is the maximum monthly profit that the factory can make given that it can produce at most $4$ items per month?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units of the product per month, the factory has a monthly revenue (income) of $R(x)=121\\cdot x=121x$ thousand dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function (in thousands of dollars) is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =121x-({x}^{4}+13x) \\\\  & =108x-{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 4.$ Therefore, $x=0$ and $x=4$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<4.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=108-4{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 108-4{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =27 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=3$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-12{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating ${P}^{″}(x)$ at the critical point, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(3)=-12(3)^{2}=-108<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that $x=3$ is a maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nOur candidates are $x=0,$ $x=3$ and $x=4.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =108(0)-{0}^{4}=0 \\\\ P(3) & =108(3)-{3}^{4}=243\\,✓ \\\\ P(4) & =108(4)-{4}^{4}=176\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum monthly profit is $$243\\,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511464 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2d19236-d6ea-57da-b2a4-b926fdddb30e" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Revenue Function Given a Price Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e7b3e86-4e95-5add-a8e0-4126a0921329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510810} {:answer/id #uuid "72f9ab55-9f87-5491-a690-8052899d1b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511021} {:answer/id #uuid "9a52c40d-9a2e-5a1c-8e75-2faaa165db38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511105} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b730d7-1e7f-50fd-818e-0b1d5da48b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511192} {:answer/id #uuid "df333b26-81a8-5312-8a45-e34486c7331f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e7b3e86-4e95-5add-a8e0-4126a0921329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510810} :answer-field/id #uuid "17a706a9-a55b-54d0-8173-3f2f3ca6d82c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511324}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13ee04f2-9803-50aa-b0c0-1f54d4ebdeb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-87569" :question/problem "An auto parts store sells $x$ units of a certain product at a price per unit $p(x),$ in dollars, given by\n\n$$\np(x)={x}^{2}-15x+63+\\frac{11}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the store sells between $1$ and $6$ units of the product each day, how many units should the store sell to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ units in total, the total revenue $R(x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =x\\cdot p(x) \\\\  & =x({x}^{2}-15x+63+\\frac{11}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-15{x}^{2}+63x+11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}-30x+63,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-30x+63 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+21 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=3$ and $x=7.$ However, the value $x=7$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only new candidate is $x=3.$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,3$ and $6.$ Computing $R(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =(1)^{3}-15(1)^{2}+63(1)+11=60 \\\\ R(3) & =(3)^{3}-15(3)^{2}+63(3)+11=92\\,✓ \\\\ R(6) & =(6)^{3}-15(6)^{2}+63(6)+11=65\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to maximize its revenue, the auto parts store should sell $3$ units."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101019 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c30919c-c2b9-5e09-8512-98fcbe339564" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Profit Function Given a Revenue and Cost Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc079cff-d7da-5a87-b324-3586bd45da20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$850$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100546} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a0efd8-50ec-59dd-9e81-0c4bfa6eebc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$640$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100547} {:answer/id #uuid "d055eaa7-a9f5-5d9e-932a-3b0feca2a7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$483.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100548} {:answer/id #uuid "f8616558-a163-56ed-b4e0-530b461b2845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$219.7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100550} {:answer/id #uuid "5dffbfce-f42c-5aa6-9041-b83e50fdad63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dffbfce-f42c-5aa6-9041-b83e50fdad63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,000$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510979} :answer-field/id #uuid "66e4fa29-0429-5e20-b824-7bf28e1113c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5396c61-6040-5997-8775-94633d636485" :question/math-academy-id "q-87573" :question/problem "A computer science center organizes a machine learning course. The course is activated if there are at least $3$ participants, and it can be attended by at most $13$ participants. The price for each participant is $$200.$ The cost of providing the course is $C(x)=200x-4{x}^{3}+0.3{x}^{4},$ where $x$ is the number of participants. What is the maximum profit that the computer science center can make?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates' test.\n\nFirst, note that for $x$ participants, the computer science center has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=200\\cdot x=200x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =200x-(200x-4{x}^{3}+0.3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-0.3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $3\\le x\\le 13.$ Therefore, $x=3$ and $x=13$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $3\\ge x\\ge 13.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{2}-1.2{x}^{3},\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-1.2{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 1.2{x}^{2}(10-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ However, the values $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=10.$\n\nLet's check if $x=10$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(x) & =24x-3.6{x}^{2} \\\\ {P}^{″}(10) & =240-360=-120<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative at  $x=10$ is negative, the point $x=10$ is a maximum. \n\nOur candidates are $x=3,x=10$ and $x=13.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =4(3)^{3}-0.3(3)^{4}=83.7 \\\\ P(10) & =4(10)^{3}-0.3(10)^{4}=1\\,000\\,✓ \\\\ P(13) & =4(13)^{3}-0.3(13)^{4}=219.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum profit is $$1\\,000.$"}]}
